note Source: Go net/textproto/reader.go, (*Reader).readContinuedLineSlice.
note It reads a logical header line, joining RFC 5322 continuation lines (lines that start
note with a space or tab) into one, with each fold replaced by a single space.

note WORKAROUND: Go's receiver r *Reader becomes an editable parameter (no methods).
note R is the wrapped bufio.Reader, a capability because reading it is I/O.
type Reader
    field r as a BufferedReader
    field buf as a Bytes

note GAP: function-typed parameter - validateFirstLine is a callback func([]byte) error that
note can also be nil. The doc has no function type and no stored callbacks. An invented
note `Validator` type with a `maybe` wrapper is used, and the call is written like an operation.
note GAP: open error set - the callback may fail with any error, which the original passes up
note unchanged. `fails with` needs a closed list of names, so the callback's failures are
note collapsed into one invented name InvalidLine.
to read_line_slice from reader within lim
    given reader is an editable Reader
    given lim is an Int
    produces Bytes
    fails with ReadError, MessageTooLarge
    uses r of reader

to skip_space in reader
    given reader is an editable Reader
    produces a Nat
    uses r of reader

to trim line
    given line is Bytes
    produces Bytes

to buffered_count of source
    given source is a BufferedReader
    produces a Nat

to peek_bytes n from source
    given n is a Nat
    given source is a BufferedReader
    produces Bytes
    uses source

to is_ascii_letter b
    given b is a Byte
    produces a Bool

to read_continued_line_slice from reader within lim checked by validate_first_line using memory
    given reader is an editable Reader
    given lim is an Int
    given validate_first_line is a maybe Validator
    given memory is an Allocator
    produces Bytes
    fails with MissingValidator, ReadError, MessageTooLarge, InvalidLine, OutOfMemory
    note UNCLEAR: the doc shows `uses` naming parameters, not a field of a parameter. The
    note capability here is the wrapped reader inside the editable Reader.
    uses r of reader, memory

    note WORKAROUND: Go returns fmt.Errorf("missing validateFirstLine func"), a formatted error
    note value. Scribe failures carry no message, so the text is lost; a named failure is used.
    var validator starts at nothing
    choose validate_first_line
        when nothing
            fail with MissingValidator
        when some given_validator
            set validator to some given_validator

    let line be try read_line_slice from reader within lim
    if count of line is 0
        note blank line - no continuation
        give back line

    note UNCLEAR: the maybe was already opened above, but the doc gives no way to keep the
    note unwrapped value outside the `when` block, so the call is made inside a second choose.
    choose validator
        when some check
            try check line
        when nothing
            fail with MissingValidator

    note Optimistically assume that the next line is already buffered and starts with an
    note ASCII letter (the next header key) or is blank, so no copy is needed.
    note GAP: peeking at buffered input - bufio.Reader.Buffered and Peek(2) have no counterpart
    note and are declared as invented operations on the BufferedReader capability. The error
    note from Peek is ignored in the original (`peek, _ :=`); here peek_bytes does not fail.
    if buffered_count of r of reader is more than 1
        let peek be peek_bytes 2 from r of reader
        note WORKAROUND: Go compares bytes with rune literals '\n' and '\r'. The doc has no
        note byte or character literal, so named constants newline_byte and return_byte are
        note assumed to exist.
        let starts_key_or_blank be count of peek is more than 0 and (is_ascii_letter (item 0 of peek) or item 0 of peek is newline_byte)
        let starts_crlf be count of peek is 2 and item 0 of peek is return_byte and item 1 of peek is newline_byte
        if starts_key_or_blank or starts_crlf
            give back trim line

    note ReadByte or the next read_line_slice will flush the read buffer; copy the slice into buf.
    note GAP: aliasing - Go's `line` is a slice into the bufio buffer that the next read will
    note overwrite, and r.buf[:0] reuses buf's storage. The doc has no slice or borrow concept,
    note so this copy, which exists only because of aliasing, has no visible meaning in Scribe.
    set buf of reader to trim line

    var remaining starts at lim
    note GAP: platform integer limit - math.MaxInt64 has no counterpart; `max_int64` is assumed.
    if remaining is less than 0
        set remaining to max_int64
    set remaining to remaining - count of buf of reader

    note WORKAROUND: Go's loop condition `r.skipSpace() > 0` both consumes input and tests it.
    note The doc forbids side effects inside expressions only for assignment, but a `repeat while`
    note condition that calls an effectful operation is not shown, so the call is moved into the
    note body with an explicit stop.
    note WORKAROUND: the doc has no unconditional loop, so a variable that never changes
    note (`more`) stands in for `repeat while true`, with `stop` as the real exit.
    note The `decreases` measure works only because buf grows by at least one byte per pass and
    note the pass fails once buf reaches remaining.
    var more starts at true
    repeat while more
        decreases remaining - count of buf of reader
        let skipped be skip_space in reader
        if skipped is 0
            stop
        note GAP: appending bytes to a byte buffer - the doc's `append value to items` adds one
        note element; `followed by` joins sequences only in ensure lines. Used here in a body.
        set buf of reader to buf of reader followed by space_byte
        if count of buf of reader is at least remaining
            fail with MessageTooLarge
        let next_line be try read_line_slice from reader within remaining - count of buf of reader
        set buf of reader to buf of reader followed by trim next_line
    give back buf of reader
